<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wends</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wends is a historical name used by Germans and others for Slavic peoples living near Germanic lands, especially between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a> applies it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> led by Samo in the 7th century, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it chiefly meant the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>, conquered and converted by German princes in the 12th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> are still sometimes called Wends.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wends.jpg" alt="Wends" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limes.saxoniae.wmt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Wends refers not to one people but to various peoples, tribes or groups, depending on where and when it was used. According to one theory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> first applied it to the ancient Veneti, who are mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and Ptolemy as inhabiting the Baltic coast. For medieval Scandinavians, Wends meant the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> living near the southern shore of the Baltic, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a>, the Rugian Slavs, the Veleti or Lutici and the Pomeranian tribes. In the south, Germans used the form Winde for the Slavs of Bavaria Slavica or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, a usage preserved in names such as Windischgraz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the first millennium CE some West Slavs moved into the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder and assimilated the remaining Germanic population. They split into many small tribes, separated by woodland and sheltered in round wooden and clay strongholds. Some joined into larger units. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> were led by dukes, while the Lutici remained independent and met in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethra">Rethra</a>. In 983 many Wend tribes took part in a great uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, which delayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> by about two centuries.</p><p>After 983 the Wends came under pressure from Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. A German expedition destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethra">Rethra</a> in 1068/69, and the Wend crusade took place in 1147. In 1168 a Danish crusade under Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">Valdemar the Great</a> captured and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> and tore down the statue of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>. With the surrender of the Rugian Wends the last independent pagan Wends were defeated. From the 12th to the 14th centuries German settlers moved into their lands in large numbers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>, and new towns were founded under German law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Wends were gradually assimilated into German-speaking culture, and the Polabian language was spoken in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> until the 17th or 18th century. Many place names in central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> are of Slavic origin, among them Delitzsch and Rochlitz, and the names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> are most likely Wendish. The Dukes of Mecklenburg, Rügen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> had Wendish ancestors. The Danish kings used the title King of the Wends from 1362, and the Swedish kings were styled kings of the Swedes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and the Wends from 1540. Both usages were dropped in 1972 and 1973. In 1854 Wends from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wends” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A ruler named Valuk appears in Fredegar as 'duke of the <b>Wends</b>' around 631.</blockquote><a href="/state/carantania">Carantania</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 15th century they used the title Prince of the <b>Wends</b>, recalling their Slavic ancestry, while their power slowly waned beside the Mecklenburg dukes.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-werle">Lordship of Werle</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 623 the Slavs of central Europe, called <b>Wends</b> in the Chronicle of Fredegar, rebelled against their Avar overlords.</blockquote><a href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a></li></ul></section>
